--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Landav distributes no third-party solver. Two are named below, and they are not
a symmetric pair: one is invoked and one has been dropped. An earlier version of
this file listed them side by side, which read as though the project depended on
both equally. It does not.

KoAT2 -- upper bounds for integer transition systems
  https://github.com/aprove-developers/KoAT2-Releases
  MIT.

  Landav invokes KoAT2 across a process boundary and nowhere else: the
  transition system is written to a file, the solver is exec'd with that file
  named on its command line, and the answer it prints is read back and parsed.
  Nothing is linked, nothing is loaded into landav's address space, and nothing
  of the solver's internals is visible to it. See crates/landav-solvers/.

  KoAT2 is not required to run landav. The native engine in
  crates/landav-engine/ derives bounds from source structure with no solver at
  all, so KoAT2's remaining role is as a differential oracle: it and the native
  engine reach the same programs by entirely different routes, so where they
  disagree one of them is wrong. Continuous integration installs KoAT2 for that
  cross-check alone -- see the `differential` job in .github/workflows/ci.yml --
  and a user who wants the same cross-check locally installs it themselves.
  Landav discovers it on PATH; it is never bundled, vendored or shipped.

LoAT -- lower bounds for integer transition systems
  GPL-3.0, forced rather than chosen by a statically linked Yices 2 and CLN.

  Dropped. Landav never invokes LoAT, does not require it, and must not be
  built or configured to link, embed or vendor it: GPL-3.0 reaching this
  repository would relicense an Apache-2.0 core and would be fatal to the
  commercial BSL 1.1 edition alongside it.

  crates/landav-solvers/src/loat_answer.rs remains in the tree and is retained
  deliberately as a parser with no caller. Reading an answer is pure, that half
  is correct and tested, and it is what an ARI emitter would need if one were
  ever written -- so it is kept rather than rewritten later. The presence of a
  reader for a tool's output is not a dependency on that tool, and is not a
  recommendation to install it. The module's own documentation says the same.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why KoAT2 is discovered on PATH and not bundled
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is a settled project decision rather than a packaging default, and it is
recorded here because the reasoning is not visible from the build files.

This project has a hard constraint of no GPL, LGPL or AGPL anywhere, including
transitively. KoAT2's own licence is MIT and raises nothing. Bundling it would,
because a distributable KoAT2 build carries APRON, which is LGPL except for its
PPL wrappers, which are GPL. Shipping someone else's binary is what turns their
dependency graph into ours; discovering a binary the user chose to install does
not.

Against that exposure, bundling buys only install convenience. It does not buy
capability: the native engine no longer needs a solver to produce a bound, so
KoAT2 is a cross-check on the shipped analysis rather than a component of it. A
convenience is not worth a licence obligation the project has committed to not
carrying.

Changing the distribution model from "discovered on PATH" to vendoring or
bundling is therefore not a packaging change and cannot be made as one. See
https://linear.app/snoodleboot/issue/LAN-7.
